  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n\u2203\\,y\\in Z,\\,\u2200x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,\u2200x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):\u2200\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,\u2203\\,y\\in Z,\\,\u2200x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\u2203\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,{{\u2200x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{\u23df}}_{P(y)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(y)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
